<article class="env-lemma" id="08RI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08RI">Lemma <span data-tag="08RI">13.9.3</span>.</a> Suppose that $f: K^\bullet \to L^\bullet $ and $g : L^\bullet \to M^\bullet $ are morphisms of complexes such that $g \circ f$ is homotopic to zero. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$g$ factors through a morphism $C(f)^\bullet \to M^\bullet $, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f$ factors through a morphism $K^\bullet \to C(g)^\bullet [-1]$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The assumptions say that the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ K^\bullet \ar[r]_ f \ar[d] &  L^\bullet \ar[d]^ g \\  0 \ar[r] &  M^\bullet }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       commutes up to homotopy. Since the cone on $0 \to M^\bullet $ is $M^\bullet $ the map $C(f)^\bullet \to C(0 \to M^\bullet ) = M^\bullet $ of Lemma <a href="/tag/014F" data-tag="014F">13.9.2</a> is the map in (1). The cone on $K^\bullet \to 0$ is $K^\bullet [1]$ and applying Lemma <a href="/tag/014F" data-tag="014F">13.9.2</a> gives a map $K^\bullet [1] \to C(g)^\bullet $. Applying $[-1]$ we obtain the map in (2). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>